<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Marcus Junius Brutus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/85-bce">85</a>–<a href="/years/42-bce">42 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Marcus Junius Brutus was a Roman senator who, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaius-cassius-longinus">Gaius Cassius Longinus</a>, led the conspiracy that killed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a> on the Ides of March 44 BCE. Claiming to defend the Republic, he left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and raised forces in the eastern provinces. After his defeat by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mark-antony">Mark Antony</a> and Octavian at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-philippi">Battle of Philippi</a> in 42 BCE, he took his own life.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/marcus-junius-brutus.jpg" alt="Marcus Junius Brutus" width="250" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CNG · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brutusidesobv.gif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Marcus Junius Brutus belonged to the plebeian gens Junia, whose founder, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucius-junius-brutus">Lucius Junius Brutus</a>, was decisive in the overthrow of the last Roman king and was one of the first two consuls of the Republic in 509 BCE. Brutus's father was killed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompey">Pompey</a> in 77 BCE. His mother, Servilia, was the half-sister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cato-the-younger">Cato the Younger</a> and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a>'s mistress. Some ancient sources raised the possibility that Caesar was his real father, but modern scholars largely reject this as chronologically improbable, since Caesar was only fifteen when Brutus was born.</p><p>In 54 BCE Brutus served as triumvir monetalis and issued denarii with the portraits of two ancestors honoured as defenders of liberty, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucius-junius-brutus">Lucius Junius Brutus</a> and Gaius Servilius Ahala. In 52 he wrote a pamphlet opposing demands that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompey">Pompey</a> be made dictator.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caesars-civil-war">Caesar's Civil War</a> began in January 49 BCE, Brutus sided with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompey">Pompey</a>, his father's killer, perhaps because his closest allies, among them Cato and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cicero">Cicero</a>, also did. After Pompey's defeat at Pharsalus on 9 August 48 BCE, Brutus wrote to Caesar, who welcomed him into his camp. Caesar later made him governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cisalpine-gaul">Cisalpine Gaul</a> and praetor urbanus for 44.</p><p>By autumn 45 BCE public opinion of Caesar was souring, and ancient authors report graffiti recalling <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucius-junius-brutus">Lucius Junius Brutus</a> and taunts that Marcus was failing his ancestors. In February 44 Caesar accepted the title dictator perpetuo. Brutus took a leading part in the assassination on the Ides of March (15 March) 44 BCE. Cicero brokered a settlement with an amnesty for the assassins and ratification of Caesar's acts for two years.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Brutus and Cassius left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in April 44 BCE and went east. After Octavian had a law passed retroactively making the conspirators murderers, a second civil war followed. At the two battles of Philippi in October 42 BCE the Caesarians decisively defeated their armies, and Brutus took his own life.</p><p>His name has become a byword for betrayal, and Dante placed him in the lowest circle of Hell. Others have praised him as a committed republican who fought for freedom against tyranny. Historians hold no consensus on his actions, and Ronald Syme observed that to judge Brutus because he failed is simply to judge from the results.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Marcus Junius Brutus” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Caesar crossed the Rubicon in 49 BCE, defeated Pompey at Pharsalus in 48 BCE and was made dictator for life early in 44 BCE, before senators led by <b>Brutus</b> and Cassius murdered him on the Ides of March.</blockquote><a href="/empire/roman-republic">Roman Republic · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Defeated by Mark Antony and Octavian in the second engagement at Philippi in Macedonia, <b>Marcus Junius Brutus</b>, a leader of Julius Caesar's assassins, took his own life.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-23">23 October in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in Philippi, in what is now <a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.01,24.29&amp;year=-58" aria-label="Philippi on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5144 700 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Marcus Junius Brutus"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5594 970h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5594 970h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=41.01,24.29&amp;year=-58">What was here in 58 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Yes, indeed, we must fly, but not with our feet, but with our hands.&#8221;</p><footer>Plutarch, Life of Brutus &#183; tr. John Dryden and others, rev. A. H. Clough, 1859 &#183; after the defeat at Philippi, meaning to die by his own hand; Plutarch's account &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/674/pg674-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;The Xanthians, suspecting my kindness, have made their country the grave of their despair; the Patareans, trusting themselves to me, enjoy in all points their former liberty; it is in your power to choose the judgment of the Patareans or the fortune of the Xanthians.&#8221;</p><footer>Plutarch, Life of Brutus &#183; tr. John Dryden and others, rev. A. H. Clough, 1859 &#183; a letter to the city of Patara, after the people of Xanthus had destroyed themselves rather than yield to him; Plutarch's account &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/674/pg674-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
